In this conversation, James inquired about determining new demand \( d_1 \) at price \( p_1 \) using the demand elasticity \( e \) at an initial price \( p_0 \) and demand \( d_0 \). I provided the formula linking elasticity and price changes. James suggested a simpler formulation, where demand is expressed as \( d = d_0 \cdot (p_0/p_1)^x \), and queried whether \( x \) corresponds to elasticity. I affirmed this, indicating \( x \) relates to \( e \) and can be expressed as \( d_1 = d_0 \cdot (p_0/p_1)^e \). James noted that price elasticities are normally negative, leading to the conclusion that \( x = -e \) to keep the direction of demand changes consistent with price increases, and I confirmed this adjustment.
